          <Overlay Id="4VQru2LluuuB5KUUSRkT7Q" Name="" Bounds="750,3294,4576,80">
            <p:ColorIndex p:Type="Int32">4</p:ColorIndex>
          </Overlay>
          <Pad Id="6eQMp0dyvOy9LY68j4SWia" Bounds="92,3526,515,275" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The second applied chapter. The capability case for Records - the thing Process nodes literally cannot do. The reader builds an interactive application where Particles appear, disappear, and behave in response to user input. The mouse-button trio is the centerpiece: left click creates a Particle, right click destroys one if the click is inside it, middle click clears them all.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is also the chapter where:&#xD;&#xA;- **The Particle arrives as the carrying example.** It will continue through chapters 42 and 43.&#xD;&#xA;- **Dispose is introduced** as the third reserved-color operation, lightly - with a forward pointer to chapter 45 where it gets its full demonstration.&#xD;&#xA;- **The Apply pin pattern** from chapters 23, 25, and 26 is finally recognized as &quot;calling member operations on a stored Record.&quot;&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="39iAS6DKcUvCkPcOSv6ENO" Bounds="92,3827,540,908" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**1. The Particle arrives - and is deliberately a Record.** This is the chapter where the carrying example for Part V appears. It is a Record because Part V is Records-first through chapter 41. Chapter 42 will rebuild this same Particle as a Class for comparison, which is when the Record-first commitment pays off most strongly.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**2. The mouse-button trio is the centerpiece.** Three small, focused interactions that each demonstrate one capability. Left-click for creation, right-click for destruction, middle-click for clearing. This is your handout's example, lightly adapted to fit the chapter's pacing. The reader builds an actual working interactive application.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**3. Dispose is introduced softly.** A column at the bottom, brief, with a forward pointer to chapter 45 for the proper treatment. The reader meets the third reserved-color operation and knows it exists, without the chapter taking on the burden of teaching it properly. Chapter 45 will deliver.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**4. The Apply pin recognition.** This is a small but real pedagogical move. The reader has been using Apply pins for chapters without realizing they were calling member operations on stored values. Naming this here closes a loop and reinforces that working with Records is not a new pattern - it is the pattern they already know, applied to their own types.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**5. No mention of mutability or Classes by name.** The chapter stays in Record territory entirely. Chapter 42 will name the contrast.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**6. The &quot;explicit store&quot; pattern is named twice but not labored.** Both in the Update Each Frame column and in the Apply Pin column. This is deliberate - it primes the reader for chapter 42, where the explicit-store discipline disappears for Classes. The reader needs to *feel* this pattern as part of their working life with Records so that its absence with Classes is felt as a relief and a trade-off.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**7. Length.** This is the longest chapter in Part V so far - nine columns of body text plus seven live patches. It needs to be, because it builds a complete interactive application. Comparable in scope to chapter 11 (Process Nodes) and chapter 28 (Collision), both of which are similarly substantial.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**8. What this chapter does *not* do.** It does not introduce performance considerations (chapter 44, Mutable Collections). It does not introduce mutable Particles (chapter 42, Classes and Mutability). It does not introduce a properly running Dispose with a clear firing moment (chapter 45, Objects as Process Nodes). Each of these is a future chapter's job, and chapter 41 stays focused on its own work.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="27MEXZmIQUqR3qr3edcNBe" Bounds="754,3601,401,459" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The first use case showed that Records make patches cleaner. The second use case shows that Records make patches capable of things they could not do before. In this chapter we will build an interactive application where objects appear and disappear during runtime, in response to user input.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is something a Process node cannot do. A Process is placed in your patch at edit time - you cannot drag a new Process onto the canvas while the application is running. But a Record is data. You can create a new one any time, drop it into a spread, and it lives there until something removes it. That difference is what makes Records and Classes a different kind of tool from Process nodes.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Throughout the rest of Part V we will work with a single carrying example - a Particle. The Particle will grow in capability across the next few chapters. Here it gets created, destroyed, and managed in a spread. In chapter 42 we will rebuild it as a Class and compare. In chapter 43 we will see how to scale up to many of them efficiently.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="48bg1o4g8Z3zeadbtrfLix" Bounds="1222,3615,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The Particle">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="2MHIMnyhRJxj9hFONHS4G5" Bounds="1222,3657,401,330" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="We define a Record called Particle. For this chapter it has three properties - a Position (Vector2), a Velocity (Vector2), and a Radius (Float). Position is where the Particle is on screen. Velocity is how it moves each frame. Radius is its size for drawing and for hit testing.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;We also give it operations. Create makes a new Particle at a starting Position with a chosen Velocity and Radius. Update advances the Position by the Velocity each frame - simple physics, no forces yet. Draw renders the Particle to a SkiaRenderer as a small circle.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Open the Particle definition in the Patch Explorer to see all of this in one place.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="5NCEMoEQ3AQlW1FpKphgd0" Bounds="1222,3947,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=92, y=720):** The Particle definition shown in the Patch Explorer (referenced), and a small inspection patch in the application showing Create Particle with three IOBoxes for the inputs and one output IOBox carrying a Particle.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="5JdIuKaECVFMqTDXt7wNmI" Bounds="1659,3616,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="A Spread of Particles">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="4wwXhDCDdJ9MfXQwmSzYWe" Bounds="1659,3658,399,273" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="A single Particle is not much of an application. We want a collection of them, where the collection can grow and shrink at runtime. We store the collection in a pad - a Spread&lt;Particle&gt; that persists across frames.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This pattern should feel familiar from chapter 25, where we managed mutable-feeling spreads of values. The same approach works here, just with our own data type instead of a built-in one. The pad starts empty - Spread&lt;Particle&gt; with zero elements. When the user clicks, we add a new Particle to the spread. When the user clicks on an existing one, we remove it. The spread changes, and the next frame draws what is currently in the spread.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="3moqRr6ag7FgU176e0LWVf" Bounds="1659,3940,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=592, y=620):** A pad containing a `Spread&lt;Particle&gt;`, with the pad shown explicitly and labeled. An IOBox downstream shows the current count of the spread. Initial state: empty.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="18L9tLNUeXxkW1zaoKNAu4" Bounds="2107,3616,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Left Click: Create">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="0KOo7qgRwABn9TJOvxQIrg" Bounds="2107,3658,399,207" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="When the user left-clicks, we want a new Particle to appear at the mouse position. We use a Mouse node (from chapter 27) to get the click event and the position. On the click trigger, we call Create Particle with that position and a random Velocity and Radius. Then we Add the new Particle to the spread in the pad.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The pattern is: read the current spread from the pad, add the new Particle to it, store the result back into the pad. This is the same explicit-store pattern we have been using since chapter 23 - read, modify, store - applied to our own data type.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="4LlEHNi9BcAFqno7vHKwCV" Bounds="2107,3889,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=1092, y=670):**&#xD;&#xA;- A Mouse node, with the left button output filtered to the click event.&#xD;&#xA;- A Create Particle node fed by the mouse position and randomized Velocity/Radius.&#xD;&#xA;- The pad's spread read out, the new Particle added with the Add operation, stored back to the pad.&#xD;&#xA;- A counter IOBox showing the current spread length.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="6aXsczEEV9ePhgwYsGRErQ" Bounds="2552,3617,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Right Click: Destroy">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="4F4CACGUnvLQ1GMGyxsr3x" Bounds="2552,3659,401,258" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="When the user right-clicks, we want to remove the Particle under the cursor - if there is one. This requires two steps: figure out which Particle the click was inside, and if there is one, remove it from the spread.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;For each Particle in the spread, we check whether the mouse position is within Radius distance of its Position. This is a simple Hit Test using the Distance node from chapter 34. If we find a hit, we get its index in the spread, then use RemoveSliceAt to take it out.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;If the click misses all Particles, nothing happens. The spread is unchanged.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="4RDVnm7kWsWdDTLlVq7d8m" Bounds="2552,3953,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=92, y=1220):**&#xD;&#xA;- Right mouse click event from Mouse node.&#xD;&#xA;- For each Particle, compute distance from click position to Particle position, compare with Radius.&#xD;&#xA;- Find the index of the first hit (TryGetFirst or similar).&#xD;&#xA;- If a hit was found, RemoveSliceAt the index, store back.&#xD;&#xA;- The spread count visibly drops by one when a Particle is clicked.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="0tmffxT9sStUUExOP6FW17" Bounds="2999,3614,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Middle Click: Clear">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="6VNW04EAwCb2Zn2OwqnEyP" Bounds="2999,3656,399,173" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The simplest of the three. On middle-click, we replace the spread in the pad with an empty spread. All Particles disappear at once.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is a useful pattern to know: sometimes the right move is not to remove a specific item, but to discard the entire collection and start fresh. In a sketch, in a demo, in any application where you want a quick reset, replacing the spread with an empty one is the cleanest way to do it.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="1fjjdOKvxb4mFzouQ6DaG8" Bounds="2999,3851,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=592, y=1140):**&#xD;&#xA;- Middle mouse click event.&#xD;&#xA;- On trigger, write an empty Spread&lt;Particle&gt; to the pad.&#xD;&#xA;- The spread count drops to zero, all Particles vanish from screen.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="1Ho1QGsYtbmJApfie4S7gK" Bounds="3423,3602,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Update Each Frame">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="1KTUKH2jtcCYUmWF8In8ka" Bounds="3423,3644,394,277" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The mouse input handles creation and destruction. We also need every Particle to update and draw every frame.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;We read the spread from the pad, ForEach over it, and inside the ForEach we call Update Particle (which advances Position by Velocity) and then Draw Particle (which renders it to a SkiaRenderer). After the ForEach, we store the updated spread back into the pad. Without the store, the updates would be lost between frames - the spread in the pad would always be the original positions.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is the explicit-store pattern again. Read, modify each item, store back. The Records have to be put back where they came from for the changes to persist.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="3Qc6WA4JeXnlWr6CxGYq0E" Bounds="3423,3943,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=1092, y=1240):**&#xD;&#xA;- Read spread from pad.&#xD;&#xA;- ForEach: Update Particle → updated Particle out → Draw Particle (which produces a Skia layer).&#xD;&#xA;- All updated Particles collected back into a new spread.&#xD;&#xA;- Store back into the pad.&#xD;&#xA;- All Skia layers grouped and rendered to the screen.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="0tnd6SeXRYFc3Eh6BPkbf4" Bounds="3866,3601,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="A Note on Dispose">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="2qR8XhgwLqgVSQ1n1J5cM2" Bounds="3866,3643,394,277" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Records and Classes can have a third reserved-color operation called Dispose. It runs automatically when an instance is destroyed. In our application, when a Particle is removed from the spread via RemoveSliceAt, its Dispose operation fires (if defined) before the instance goes away.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is useful when an object owns something that needs to be cleaned up - a file handle, a network connection, a sound that should fade out. For our simple Particle there is nothing to clean up, so we leave Dispose out for now.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;We will come back to Dispose in chapter 45, where we look at it more carefully - including the subtle question of when exactly it fires for Records versus Classes.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="2dwdSvCOtQXCOGA26p9edH" Bounds="4326,3616,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The Apply Pin Pattern, Recognized">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="2dNMViN08nbGHzFOmherYK" Bounds="4326,3658,394,277" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="A small recognition worth naming. Throughout this chapter we have used a pattern that has appeared in every chapter since chapter 23 - read a value from a pad, modify it via an operation, store it back. With built-in types like Spread&lt;RGBA&gt; in chapter 25 or Dictionary in chapter 26, we used Apply pins to do this in one step.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The same applies to Records. The Apply pin pattern is essentially &quot;call this operation on the value stored here and store the result back.&quot; Whether the value is a built-in Spread or our own Particle, the pattern is the same. You were already calling member operations on stored values; you just did not have your own data types to do it with.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Overlay Id="6weg3s5y5hfEIaUTcSn1Ta" Bounds="4996,3764,383,298">
            <p:ColorIndex p:Type="Int32">10</p:ColorIndex>
          </Overlay>
